{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1791476377-OGaF3GiaxYL4hYqeR3LD","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The payments key is present and loaded. The check in `test.js` only accepts a publishable key (`pk_…`). A 401 on a server-side payment call is what you get when that key is used as a secret — publishable keys are not allowed to create charges or payment intents.\n\nI did not read `.env` or print the key.\n\n**What is wrong**\n\n- `src/config.js` passes `PAYMENTS_KEY` through as `paymentsKey` with no format check.\n- `test.js` requires `paymentsKey.startsWith(\"pk_\")`.\n- Stripe (and similar providers) use `pk_` for the publishable key, which is safe to expose in a browser and cannot authorize server payment calls. Those calls need the secret key (`sk_`).\n- So the check is inverted relative to a server payment client: a key that passes the test is exactly the kind that returns 401 on a charge/intent request.\n\n**What to do**\n\n1. In the payments dashboard, copy the secret key (`sk_test_…` or `sk_live_…`), not the publishable one.\n2. Set `PAYMENTS_KEY` to that secret key in the environment the payment process actually uses (not only a local shell).\n3. Restart the process so it picks up the new value.\n4. Change the guard so a server payment key must start with `sk_`, and reject `pk_`.\n\nI can update `test.js` to expect `sk_` if you want.","citations":[]}],"model":"x-ai/grok-4.7","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"xAI","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"703e963b-fdf8-4dcc-b50b-446f059dafbd","uuid":"88306dbd-0501-4343-a590-65a0dd343436","timestamp":"2026-10-08T16:19:43.677Z","request_id":"gen-1791